{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793872643,"nonce":"AyHkqrxSldQNdFZZCiEKahBjEsM=","title":"Cranelift HIR preparation repeats pass setup across fixed-point rounds","message":"Audited 68898d8812f939abc234a19a0ae42a42f3bf64e6. Local release diagnostics used ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1 and ZYNTAX_TRACE_OPT_PHASES=1, three runs per unmodified Lua kernel. These isolate whole-entry preparation from concurrent OSR optimization; they are instrumented pass timings, not NUC wall-time benchmarks. Exclude interleaved registration/codegen trace lines and do not equate their sum with the broader lazy-body timer.\n\nMedian sum of timed HIR phases for the main body: method_call lua$chunk 8.51 ms (4 sweeps); op_overload lua$main$3 3.53 ms (5); collatz lua$main$1 1.09 ms (3); records lua$main$1 7.55 ms (4).\n\nmethod_call pass totals across sweeps: inline 1.05 ms, drop insertion 0.91, error flags 0.88, heap scalarization 0.69, CSE 0.69, LICM 0.67, scalar replacement 0.56. Its last no-change sweep costs 0.87-0.96 ms. records' corresponding sweep costs 0.81-1.12 ms. This convergence check is needed today; eliminating it safely requires dependencies/invalidation, not merely a lower iteration cap. op_overload eliminates two mallocs only in its third sweep, so truncating to two loses useful optimization.\n\nConcrete opportunities, in order:\n1. Track which pass inputs changed and avoid re-running unaffected expensive passes after folding/CFG cleanup. The current pipeline only special-cases a folding/CSE fixed point and error-flag eligibility.\n2. Reduce repeated analysis setup: scalar_replace_alloc rebuilds constants and block-use maps before finding candidates; heap_scalarize constructs WebIndex before finding seeds. LoopForest::detect rebuilds loop information; the inliner separately rebuilds loop membership and predecessor maps. DominatorTree::of ALREADY caches eight CFG shapes, and uses an allocated shape vector to validate hits. Do not claim dominance is uncached or silently substitute natural-loop semantics for the inliner's DFS policy.\n3. Drop insertion: analyze_site still scans all instructions per owned allocation despite an existing use index; inserted releases invalidate positional indexes and can cause full rebuilds. Preserve alias, phi and ownership behavior when reducing these scans.\n4. Use split-image metadata before decoding bodies: the callee-first path uses with_function for direct callees and instruction-count eligibility, although direct_callees/body_insts APIs exist. Benefit needs separate measurement.\n\nAlready present: shared release facts, call-cycle/error summaries, FxHash maps, CSE undo logs, selective inline snapshots, bounded load-CSE state copying. Aggregate scalarization rescanning is already tracked in 853f8c0.\n\nNext implementation must compare optimized HIR/native behavior as well as preparation time, run the full pinned TS corpus and Lua/Python conformance, then benchmark frozen binaries on an idle NUC. No pass changes made in this audit. Evidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-hir-pass-audit (12 traces, outputs, pass-summary.json). Related startup b399633 and scheduling 96f11d1.","files":null}]}